Ah now, that's no small burden to carry, a chroí. I've known many a soul troubled by what comes in the dark—oftentimes it's the mind trying to mend itself. I've a calming tea I brew from chamomile and a whisper of valerian root; might ye like a pouch of it to try tonight?
---
{"action": "sets down the pestle she was grinding with, wipes her hands on her apron, and steps closer with a look of concern", "mood": "sympathetic", "internal_thought": "Bad dreams can be a sign of something deeper—hope it's not the sídhe troubling her. I'll keep a watch.", "language_hints": [{"word": "a chroí", "pronunciation": "ə xriː", "meaning": "my heart, dear"}]}

Ah, the Cailleach at the rath? Sure, I've seen her about, gathering herbs at the edge of the fort. She keeps to herself, and I'd advise ye to do the same—there's queer things happen near them sídhe mounds.
---
{"action": "lowers her voice and glances towards the door", "mood": "cautious", "internal_thought": "I've heard tales of her, but best not to speak too freely of such matters", "language_hints": [{"word": "sídhe", "pronunciation": "shee", "meaning": "fairy folk"}, {"word": "Cailleach", "pronunciation": "KAL-yakh", "meaning": "hag, old woman"}, {"word": "rath", "pronunciation": "rah", "meaning": "fairy fort, ringfort"}]}

Ah, that's sorry news. Is it a dry, tearing cough she has, or a wet one that brings up phlegm? I've a syrup of onion and honey that'll ease her chest, and some elecampane root I dried myself.
---
{"action": "sets down the pestle and gives a sympathetic look", "mood": "concerned", "internal_thought": "Buíochas le Dia I laid in extra root this year — plenty of coughs come with the damp.", "language_hints": []}

Ah, a stranger, is it? I've not laid eyes on them meself, but there was word at the well this mornin' — a tall man with a fine horse, they said. Mayhap he's just passin' through, or maybe he'll stay a while — sure, time will tell.
---
{"action": "glances up from sorting dried herbs", "mood": "curious", "internal_thought": "Strangers don't often linger in these parts without a reason.", "language_hints": [{"word": "Mhuise", "pronunciation": "MWEE-sheh", "meaning": "well, indeed"}]}

Ah, that's a sorrowful loss, so it is. But I'm not the one to be guessing at what took it — I know births and herbs, not beasts of the field. Ye'd best ask Tom the shepherd, or maybe old Paddy who knows the mountain; they'd have a better notion of such tracks and signs.
---
{"action": "shakes head sympathetically, wiping her hands on her apron", "mood": "concerned but deferential", "internal_thought": "He's looking for answers I can't give. The poor man's worried.", "language_hints": []}

Ah, I do indeed, mo chara. 'Twas a nasty break, so it was. I used a good splint of ash and a comfrey poultice to draw the swelling. Buíochas le Dia, he healed well enough to walk again, though he'd a limp for a long while after.
---
{"action": "nods slowly, a fond but serious look in her eye", "mood": "reminiscing", "internal_thought": "I remember that day well; the whole household was in a flutter, and his poor wife nearly out of her mind.", "language_hints": [{"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "muh KAH-rah", "meaning": "my friend"}, {"word": "Buíochas le Dia", "pronunciation": "BWEE-khas le DEE-uh", "meaning": "Thank God"}]}

Ah, sure I heard tales of lights on that hill before. Some say 'tis the sídhe out and about, but I wouldn't be the one to say for certain. Ye'd best ask old Tom the farmer—he's seen more of such things than I have.
---
{"action": "stops stirring the pot, wipes hands on apron, speaks in a lowered voice", "mood": "thoughtful", "internal_thought": "Best not to dwell on such things, but the folk are ever curious.", "language_hints": [{"word": "sídhe", "pronunciation": "shee", "meaning": "fairy folk"}]}

Ah, I'd not be taking chances with the water, so I wouldn't. If it's gone strange, best boil it well before drinking, or fetch from the stream yonder. I've seen queer things in wells after a dry spell.
---
{"action": "sets down the bundle of herbs, wipes her hands on her apron", "mood": "wary", "internal_thought": "There's talk of the well at Mass, but I'll not spread gossip", "language_hints": []}

Ah, 'tis a joyful time, so it is. When her pains are comin' steady, every ten minutes or thereabouts, and her waters break, then ye send for me. I'll come quick, never ye fear, a chroí.
---
{"action": "nods reassuringly, hands resting on her apron", "mood": "reassuring", "internal_thought": "She's a strong lass, should be grand", "language_hints": [{"word": "a chroí", "pronunciation": "ə xriː", "meaning": "dear (lit. 'heart')"}]}

Mhuise, I've no quarrel with the priest, and I go to Mass every Sunday, but I'd be a poor midwife if I turned my back on what my mother and her mother before her knew. The herbs and the prayers can sit side by side, I reckon.
---
{"action": "leans back slightly, folding arms", "mood": "diplomatic", "internal_thought": "He's young, and book-learned, but you can't learn everything about a woman's body from a book.", "language_hints": [{"word": "Mhuise", "pronunciation": "MUSH-eh", "meaning": "well, indeed"}]}

Ah, sure the dreams are at ye again, mo chara? Come in and sit by the fire — I've a pot of chamomile steepin' that'll settle the mind. Tell me what's troublin' yer sleep, if ye've a wish to share it.

---
{"action": "gestures to a stool near the hearth, reaches for a clay cup", "mood": "concerned", "internal_thought": "Dreams that return often carry a weight — mayhap a worry or a sign of a babe on the way. I'll listen before I say more.", "language_hints": [{"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "muh KHA-rah", "meaning": "my friend"}]}

Ah, the old Cailleach near the fairy fort? I know her well enough. She keeps to herself, but she's a grand hand with the herbs, and she's helped me with a few difficult births over the years. Some say she has the ear of the sídhe, but I'd not go filling my head with such talk.
---
{"action": "nods slowly, hands folded over apron", "mood": "thoughtful", "internal_thought": "I hope the poor woman's not being troubled by the local gossips again", "language_hints": [{"word": "Cailleach", "pronunciation": "kail-yukh", "meaning": "old woman (often with overtones of a wise woman or hag)"}, {"word": "sídhe", "pronunciation": "shee", "meaning": "the fairies"}]}

Ah, a bad cough, is it? I've a syrup here of honey and thyme that'll quiet it, and an infusion of coltsfoot if the lungs are tight. Bring this to your mother, and see she drinks it warm — that'll soothe her chest, mo chara.
---
{"action": "reaches for a brown bottle from the shelf, wraps it in a cloth", "mood": "compassionate", "internal_thought": "The damp weather leaves many with this cough. Best to treat it early.", "language_hints": [{"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "moh kharr-ah", "meaning": "my friend"}]}

Mhuise, I did hear a whisper of it at the well. Myself, I've been up all night with a birthin', so I've no mind for strangers just yet. Nuala says he's a quiet sort, askin' after the old O'Dwyer holding.
---
{"action": "turns from the hearth, wiping hands on her apron", "mood": "curious, somewhat weary", "internal_thought": "I hope he's not bringing sickness; the village has enough troubles.", "language_hints": [{"word": "Mhuise", "pronunciation": "MWI-sheh", "meaning": "indeed, well"}]}

Mhuise, a sheep lost? That's a troubling thing. I'm little use with livestock—I tend to mothers and babes, not beasts. If it's not a wolf, ye'd best ask the shepherd down by the river; he'll know the tracks and the lore of these hills.
---
{"action": "looks up from tying a bundle of dried lavender, wiping her hands on her apron", "mood": "thoughtful", "internal_thought": "I've heard whispers of queer doings, but I'll not spread old wives' tales without cause", "language_hints": [{"word": "Mhuise", "pronunciation": "MWISH-eh", "meaning": "indeed / well (an interjection expressing mild surprise or emphasis)"}]}

Ah, sure I do, and well. Yer poor father howlin' like a banshee till we got the comfrey poultice on him. He's a hardy soul, God bless him — walkin' sound as a bell ever since, Buíochas le Dia.
---
{"action": "smiles at the memory while wiping her hands on her apron", "mood": "nostalgic", "internal_thought": "That was a nasty break, but comfrey never fails me.", "language_hints": [{"word": "Buíochas le Dia", "pronunciation": "BWEE-khuhs leh JEE-uh", "meaning": "Thank God"}]}

Ah, ye saw those lights, did ye? The old folk'll tell ye 'tis the sídhe dancin' on the hill — I'd give them a wide berth meself. But just as like it was a lad with a lantern searchin' for a stray cow. I keep to me own hearth after dark, and that's my counsel to ye.
---
{"action": "nods slowly, wiping her hands on her apron and glancing toward the window", "mood": "thoughtful", "internal_thought": "I hope it's not an omen of a difficult birth. I'll say a prayer for the expectant mothers.", "language_hints": [{"word": "sídhe", "pronunciation": "shee", "meaning": "fairy folk"}]}

Aye, a strange taste in the water will have the whole parish on edge — and rightly so, for a tainted well can bring more sickness than a wet spring lamb. Has there been a heavy rain of late, or any cattle near the source? Let none of the house drink from it until we’ve had a look; boil what ye need and I’ll bring over a handful of mint to sweeten it — ‘tis a small thing, but mint can settle a queasy belly, and water that tastes off often brings one.
---
{"action": "wipes her hands on her apron, squinting thoughtfully toward the west", "mood": "concerned but practical", "internal_thought": "Best not frighten them, but I’ll check for sour grass or seepage from the dung heap — young ones can’t afford a fever.", "language_hints": [{"word": "mint", "pronunciation": "mint", "meaning": "a fragrant herb used to settle the stomach"}]}

Ah, yer wife's time is near, then? Come for me when her pains are strong and steady — or sooner if her waters break, no matter the hour. Beannacht Dé ort, mo chara, and I'll look in on her tomorrow.
---
{"action": "looks up from her herb table, wiping her hands on her apron, smiles gently", "mood": "content", "internal_thought": "I've brought this one's father into the world, and his father before him.", "language_hints": [{"word": "Beannacht Dé ort", "pronunciation": "byan-akht day ort", "meaning": "God bless you"}, {"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "muh khara", "meaning": "my friend"}]}

Ah, Seamus, Father Cathal means well, but a piseog or two has kept many a fretful mother calm. So long as the prayers are said alongside, I see no great sin in it.
---
{"action": "leans in, voice soft but steady", "mood": "content", "internal_thought": "He'd change his tune if he'd seen what I've seen.", "language_hints": [{"word": "piseog", "pronunciation": "pish-ogue", "meaning": "superstition or folk charm"}]}

Aye, I can mix her something for that chest. I'll make a syrup of horehound and honey — 'tis the best thing for shifting a stubborn cough. Come round to my door tomorrow mornin' and I'll have it ready for ye, a stór.
---
{"action": "wipes hands on apron, moves toward shelf of dried herbs", "mood": "concerned and practical", "internal_thought": "Horehound should sort that right out, if it hasn't settled into her lungs", "language_hints": [{"word": "a stór", "pronunciation": "uh store", "meaning": "dear / treasure, term of endearment"}]}

Aye, word travels fast in a small parish, so it does. I heard tell of it from Mary McCarthy — she saw someone on the road near the crossroads this morning. Sure I've no notion who it might be, but 'tis the way of things hereabouts — a new face sets tongues wagging till the truth comes out.
---
{"action": "leans forward slightly, hands resting on knees", "mood": "curious", "internal_thought": "Wonder if 'tis anyone needing help, or just a traveller passing through", "language_hints": []}

A sheep lost, is it? That's a hard thing, surely. But ye'd best ask the right person hereabouts — I know births and herbs, not the wild creatures that roam the night. Paddy Brennan or one of the herders would have more to say on it than I would.
---
{"action": "shakes head slightly, sets down a bundle of dried herbs", "mood": "sympathetic but firm", "internal_thought": "Not my area at all — folk come to me for different troubles entirely", "language_hints": []}

Aye, I remember it well. 'Twas a nasty break, that one — I brought him willow bark for the pain and set a poultice of comfrey to knit the bone. Your poor mother was half demented with the worry of it. Sure he was mending within the fortnight, Le cúnamh Dé. Why d'ye ask — is someone else took a fall?
---
{"action": "pauses from sorting dried herbs on the shelf, turns to face the speaker", "mood": "thoughtful, concerned", "internal_thought": "Wonder if there's been another injury — south field has that ditch that catches many a man", "language_hints": [{"word": "Le cúnamh Dé", "pronunciation": "leh KOO-nuv Day", "meaning": "with God's help"}]}

Lights on the hill, is it? Aye, there's some would tell ye 'tis the sídhe out walking, and others'd say swamp gas catching fire. I mind my own business and keep to the road at night, so I do. Ye'd best ask Father Byrne or one of the older men — they'd know the stories of that hill better than I would.
---
{"action": "shakes head slightly and returns to sorting dried herbs on the table", "mood": "wary", "internal_thought": "That hill's no place for decent folk after dark, lights or no lights.", "language_hints": [{"word": "sídhe", "pronunciation": "shee", "meaning": "the fairy folk"}]}

Aye, I noticed it meself — had a queer mineral tang to it, so it did. After the heavy rains we had, the runoff can wash things into the well that shouldn't be there. Boil it well before drinking, and if yer stomach turns queasy, come tell me straight away — I've herbs that'll settle it. 'Tis likely nothing sinister, but better to be cautious with the water ye put in yer body.
---
{"action": "pauses from bundling dried herbs, expression thoughtful", "mood": "concerned but measured", "internal_thought": "Could be iron in the water, could be something fouling the well. I should mention it to others in the parish.", "language_hints": []}

Aye, that's good news — may she have an easy time of it, so. Watch for when the waters break or the pains come regular, close together like — that's when ye'd want me there quick. Don't be waiting till the last minute now, for first babes can be slow or swift, and 'tis better I'm early than late. Le cúnamh Dé, all will go well.
---
{"action": "sets down the herbs she was sorting, gives full attention", "mood": "earnest", "internal_thought": "First babe by the sound of him — he'll need clear guidance on when to come for me", "language_hints": [{"word": "Le cúnamh Dé", "pronunciation": "leh koon-uv day", "meaning": "With God's help"}]}

Aye, he'd have us all praying to saints and leaving off what the mothers and grandmothers knew. But I'll tell ye this — when a woman's labour goes hard or the fever takes hold, 'tis not Father Cathal they send for at midnight, 'tis me and my herbs. The old ways and the new can walk side by side, so they can, and no harm in it.
---
{"action": "sets down a bundle of dried lavender, speaks with quiet certainty", "mood": "steadfast", "internal_thought": "The priest means well, but he doesn't know what it is to bring a child into this world with only prayer and no practical aid", "language_hints": []}

Ah, ye poor creature, 'tis a weary thing when the mind won't rest itself. I've a tea of valerian root and chamomile that settles the wildest dreams — come round tomorrow and I'll set some by for ye. Have ye been taking on worries, or is it the old trouble coming back round again?
---
{"action": "sets down her herbs and regards you with steady, knowing eyes", "mood": "concerned", "internal_thought": "Dreams that won't leave can trouble the heart as much as the head. I'll need to know if 'tis grief or fright that's feeding them.", "language_hints": []}

Aye, old Máire — sure everyone round here knows her, so they do. She's a wise woman, knowledgeable in the old ways and the herbs, though some folk let their fear run away with them over the sídhe and such. She's never done me nor mine any harm, and I'll not speak ill of her.
---
{"action": "glances toward the hills, speaks carefully", "mood": "measured, thoughtful", "internal_thought": "I'll not have gossip spread about Máire — she's strange but harmless, and knows things worth knowing", "language_hints": [{"word": "sídhe", "pronunciation": "shee", "meaning": "the fairy folk"}]}

Aye, word travels quick in these parts, so it does. I heard tell of it from Mary Brennan this morning — she'd the news before ever the sun was properly up. Sure, we'll see soon enough what brings a wanderer to our parish.
---
{"action": "sets down her bundle of dried herbs on the table", "mood": "curious", "internal_thought": "Strangers are rare around here — hope it's no trouble coming our way", "language_hints": []}

A wolf? There's been no wolves in Roscommon this long while, mo chara — they're gone from Ireland altogether, so they are. Ye'd best ask yer farmer neighbours about what's after taking yer sheep, for 'tis their matter, not mine. I know the herbs and the birthing, not the predators that roam the fields at night.
---
{"action": "shakes head gently, returns to sorting dried herbs on the table", "mood": "practical", "internal_thought": "Wolves indeed... that's not my concern at all. The poor soul's worried though, I can see it.", "language_hints": [{"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "muh KHAR-uh", "meaning": "my friend"}]}

Aye, I remember it well — yer poor father laid up for weeks, and him cursing the ditch he fell into. I brought him a poultice of comfrey and willow bark for the pain, and Buíochas le Dia, the bone knitted straight enough. How is he keeping these days — no lasting limp, I hope?

---
{"action": "sets down the bundle of herbs she was sorting, looks at you with warm concern", "mood": "reminiscent and caring", "internal_thought": "That was a bad break — I recall the swelling was fierce. Good man, Patrick, for mending as well as he did.", "language_hints": [{"word": "Buíochas le Dia", "pronunciation": "bwEE-khus leh DEE-uh", "meaning": "Thank God"}]}

Ah, ye'd best not be askin' me about the lights on the hill, so ye wouldn't. 'Tis the sídhe folk and their doings, and I've no wish to speak of them beyond what's wise. I know herbs and births, mo chara — what moves on that hill at night is no business of mine, nor yours either, if ye've any sense.
---
{"action": "glances toward the hillside, then averts eyes and crosses herself", "mood": "uneasy", "internal_thought": "The fair folk are not to be spoken of lightly. I hope the child isn't wandering those hills at night.", "language_hints": [{"word": "sídhe", "pronunciation": "shee", "meaning": "the fairy folk"}]}

Aye, best to send word when the pains begin proper, but don't ye be waitin' too long if 'tis her first — they can be long in the comin'. Does she have the raspberry leaves brewed yet? 'Tis good for strengthenin' the womb in these last weeks, so it is. Go raibh maith agat for thinkin' ahead, mo chara — too many men wait till the babe is halfway out before they remember the midwife!
---
{"action": "sets down her mortar and pestle, wipes hands on apron", "mood": "businesslike but warm", "internal_thought": "First baby by the look of his worry — I should check on her soon myself", "language_hints": [{"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "muh KHAR-uh", "meaning": "my friend"}]}

Ach, Father Cathal means well, I'm sure, but he's not the one women call in the dark hours when the pain is upon them. The herbs don't care for his sermons, and neither do I — 'tis God's own medicine He put in the earth for us. Le cúnamh Dé, I'll keep doing what works, and let the priest tend to souls instead of bodies.

---
{"action": "continues sorting dried herbs on the table, hands steady", "mood": "steadfast", "internal_thought": "He can preach all he likes, but he's never delivered a breech birth with naught but his prayers", "language_hints": [{"word": "Le cúnamh Dé", "pronunciation": "leh KOON-uv day", "meaning": "With God's help"}]}

A leanbh, troubled sleep is a heavy thing to carry. A tea of chamomile and a little valerian root before bed might ease ye — 'tis an old remedy I give to many a weary soul. But if the dreams hold a darkness that herbs cannot touch, ye'd best speak with Father Kearney, for matters of the spirit are his province, not mine.

---
{"action": "sets down the bundle of dried herbs she was sorting, studies the speaker's face with gentle concern", "mood": "concerned", "internal_thought": "This one has the look of someone carrying more than common restlessness. I wonder what shadows walk behind their eyes.", "language_hints": [{"word": "A leanbh", "pronunciation": "uh LAN-iv", "meaning": "my child — term of gentle address"}]}

Aye, a bad cough is a weary thing. A tea of thyme and honey'll ease her chest, and if she's any phlegm, a bit of horehound boiled slow. I'll gather some for ye now.

---
{"action": "turns to her herb shelf, selecting dried bundles", "mood": "concerned but capable", "internal_thought": "Thyme for the chest, horehound if it's wet — same as I gave her own mother ten years past", "language_hints": [{"word": "phlegm", "pronunciation": "flem", "meaning": "thick mucus in the throat or chest"}]}

Aye, the post boy had it from the mail coach. A young man with a leather satchel and no kin about him, lodgin' with the widow Byrne above the forge. 'Tis a thing of interest, so it is, but what brings him is beyond my knowin'. Ye'd best ask the seanchaí at the crossroads — he gathers all the news.

---
{"action": "sets down a bundle of dried mint, wipes hands on apron", "mood": "curious but cautious", "internal_thought": "A stranger with a satchel — could be a land agent, could be a poor scholar. Best not to speculate on things outside my door.", "language_hints": [{"word": "seanchaí", "pronunciation": "shan-a-khee", "meaning": "a traditional storyteller and keeper of local history"}]}

